 months of fiscal year 2026, goodwill increased by $\n1.1\nbillion from acquisitions and was allocated to our Compute & Networking reporting unit.\nNote 8 -\nBalance Sheet Components\nWe refer to customers who purchase products directly from NVIDIA as direct customers, such as add-in board manufacturers, distributors, original device manufacturers, or ODMs, original equipment manufacturers, or OEMs, cloud service providers, or CSPs, hyperscale companies, and system integrators. Certain direct customers may use either internal resources or third-party system integrators to complete their build. Four direct customers accounted for\n22\n%,\n17\n%,\n14\n% and\n12\n% of our accounts receivable balance as of October 26, 2025. Two direct customers accounted for\n17\n% and\n16\n% of our accounts receivable balance as of January 26, 2025.\nCertain balance sheet components are as follows:\nOct 26, 2025\nJan 26, 2025\nInventories:\n(In millions)\nRaw materials\n$\n4,209\n$\n3,408\nWork in process\n8,735\n3,399\nFinished goods\n6,840\n3,273\nTotal inventories (1)\n$\n19,784\n$\n10,080\n(1)    We recorded inventory provisions of $\n378\nmillion and $\n322\nmillion for the third quarter of fiscal years 2026 and 2025, respectively, and $\n3.6\nbillion and $\n876\nmillion for the first nine months of fiscal years 2026 and 2025, respectively, in cost of revenue.\nProperty and Equipment:\nProperty, equipment and intangible assets acquired but not paid for the first nine months of fiscal years 2026 and 2025 were $\n790\nmillion and not significant, respectively.\nOct 26, 2025\nJan 26, 2025\nOther Assets (Long Term):\n(In millions)\nNon-marketable equity securities\n$\n8,187\n$\n3,387\nPrepaid supply and capacity agreements (1)\n1,536\n1,747\nIncome tax receivable\n1,369\n750\nOther\n632\n541\nTotal other assets\n$\n11,724\n$\n6,425\n(1)\n$\n2.0\nbillion and $\n3.3\nbillion were included in short-term Prepaid expenses and other current assets as of October 26, 2025 and January 26, 2025, respectively.\n15\nNVIDIA CORPORATION AND SUBSIDIARIES\nNOTES TO CONDENSED CONSOLIDATED FINANCIAL STATEMENTS (Continued)\n(Unaudited)\nOct 26, 2025\nJan 26, 2025\nAccrued and Other Current Liabilities:\n(In millions)\nCustomer program accruals\n$\n4,721\n$\n4,880\nExcess inventory purchase obligations (1)\n2,770\n2,095\nProduct warranty\n2,707\n1,290\nTaxes payable\n2,915\n881\nDeferred revenue (2)\n1,248\n837\nAccrued payroll and related expenses\n895\n848\nOther\n1,196\n906\nTotal accrued and other current liabilities\n$\n16,452\n$\n11,737\n(1)    We recorded $\n32\nmillion and $\n543\nmillion for the third quarter of fiscal years 2026 and 2025, respectively, and $\n3.1\nbillion and $\n1.3\nbillion for the first nine months of fiscal years 2026 and 2025, respectively, in cost of revenue.\n(2)    Includes customer advances and unearned revenue related to hardware and software support, cloud	{"summary": "NVIDIA reported exceptional Q3 FY2026 results with revenue of $57.0 billion, up 62% year-over-year, driven primarily by explosive Data Center demand for AI accelerators. Net income reached $31.9 billion for the quarter and $77.1 billion for the first nine months of FY2026, with diluted EPS of $1.30 and $3.14 respectively. The balance sheet strengthened significantly with total assets growing to $161.1 billion and shareholders' equity of $118.9 billion, while the company returned $36.7 billion to shareholders through buybacks year-to-date.", "key_risks": ["Customer and geographic concentration risk: a significant portion of revenue is concentrated among a handful of hyperscale customers and Taiwan-based intermediaries, with four customers comprising 22%, 17%, 14%, and 12% of accounts receivable", "Export control and geopolitical risk: U.S. restrictions on sales to China and other regions could materially impact revenue, particularly in the Compute & Networking segment which depends heavily on AI infrastructure spending", "Supply chain and inventory risk: inventory surged to $19.8 billion from $10.1 billion, inventory provisions reached $3.6 billion for the first nine months, and excess purchase obligations of $2.8 billion reflect significant exposure if demand softens"], "key_opportunities": ["Accelerating AI infrastructure buildout: Data Center revenue continues to grow rapidly as hyperscalers and enterprises deploy next-generation GPU clusters, with the Compute & Networking segment driving the vast majority of revenue and profit", "Automotive and emerging verticals: Automotive revenue is scaling quickly, and diversification into robotics, agentic AI, and sovereign AI programs globally opens large incremental addressable markets beyond core cloud data center", "Expanding ecosystem and software monetization: growing non-marketable equity portfolio ($8.2 billion), strategic investments in AI companies like Anthropic, and software/services deferred revenue provide compounding revenue streams beyond hardware"], "financial_health": "NVIDIA's financial condition is outstanding, with $60.6 billion in combined cash, equivalents, and marketable securities, operating cash flow of $66.5 billion for the first nine months, and a lean debt load of approximately $8.5 billion, giving the company exceptional liquidity and capital return capacity. Gross margins remain robust and the company is aggressively investing in R&D and supply capacity while still generating industry-leading free cash flow.", "sentiment_score": 9}	# NVIDIA Corporation (NVDA) — Institutional Investment Brief\n**Based on Q3 FY2026 10-Q Filing | Current Price: $211.14**\n\n---\n\n## Trajectory: Structural AI Dominance Intact, Execution Risk Emerging\n\nNVIDIA's Q3 FY2026 10-Q confirms the company remains the indispensable infrastructure layer of the global AI buildout, not merely a cyclical beneficiary. Revenue of $57.0 billion, up 62% year-over-year, with net income of $31.9 billion in a single quarter, represents one of the most concentrated profit-generation events in the history of large-cap technology. The Data Center segment is unambiguously the engine, as hyperscalers continue to front-load capital expenditure into GPU clusters ahead of next-generation model training cycles. What the filing reveals beyond headline strength is a company deliberately stretching its balance sheet to meet demand: inventory surged to $19.8 billion from $10.1 billion, purchase obligations remain elevated, and inventory provisions of $3.6 billion year-to-date signal management is absorbing execution risk to preserve customer relationships and market share. The $60.6 billion liquidity position and $66.5 billion in nine-month operating cash flow provide an extraordinary buffer, but the inventory build is the single most important operational variable to monitor in coming quarters. The emerging Automotive vertical and sovereign AI initiatives represent credible long-duration optionality that the market has yet to fully price, while the $8.2 billion non-marketable equity portfolio in AI-adjacent companies quietly compounds NVIDIA's strategic moat beyond silicon.\n\n---\n\n## Price Action: Recovery Narrative Broadly Validates Fundamentals, but Momentum Fading\n\nThe 45-day price history tells a clear two-act story. From late March through early April, NVDA traded in a compressed $165–$183 range, reflecting macro-driven multiple compression tied to tariff escalation fears and geopolitical uncertainty around export controls — risks the 10-Q explicitly flags as material. The subsequent recovery from the $177–$183 base to a peak of $235.74 on May 14th, a roughly 33% move in five weeks, tracks closely with market stabilization and renewed confidence in hyperscaler capex commitments. However, the more recent retreat from $235 back toward $211 suggests the initial relief rally has largely run its course and the stock is now in a price-discovery phase where execution on the Blackwell ramp and forward guidance clarity will need to carry the load. The current price implies the market is still discounting some combination of China revenue risk and potential demand deceleration at the margin, a reasonable posture given that four customers account for a combined 65% of accounts receivable. The filing's financial health does not justify the March lows, but the May highs arguably priced near-perfection; the current $211 level represents a more balanced entry point for long-horizon institutional capital.\n\n---\n\n## Catalysts and Risks to Monitor: The Next 90 Days Are Binary on Two Variables\n\nThe most consequential near-term catalyst is the trajectory of U.S.–China export control policy. The 10-Q is explicit that restrictions on H20 and successor products represent a direct revenue headwind with limited short-term offset, and any further tightening — or conversely, any licensing pathway — would be an immediate material mover in either direction. Second, the Blackwell architecture ramp and associated supply chain normalization will be closely scrutinized in Q4 FY2026 guidance; the inventory build is only justified if sell-through accelerates, and any inventory correction signal would compress near-term earnings estimates sharply. On the opportunity side, hyperscaler capex guidance from Microsoft, Google, Amazon, and Meta over the next two earnings cycles functions as a leading indicator for NVIDIA's forward order book — any upward revision in AI infrastructure spending is a direct read-through. Automotive revenue scaling toward a billion-dollar quarterly run-rate and incremental sovereign AI contract announcements represent meaningful positive catalysts that are underappreciated in current consensus models. Investors should also watch gross margin trajectory closely, as supply chain investments and potential pricing concessions to defend share in a more competitive landscape could compress margins even as revenue grows.\n\n---\n\n## Investment Stance: Overweight with Disciplined Position Sizing\n\nNVIDIA remains the highest-conviction large-cap AI infrastructure holding available to institutional investors, with a financial profile — cash generation, balance sheet strength, and ecosystem lock-in — that few technology companies have achieved at this scale. The filing reinforces that the core thesis is intact and the company is investing aggressively to extend its lead rather than harvesting margins. At $211, NVDA is not cheap on traditional valuation metrics, but the magnitude and durability of its competitive position in AI accelerators, combined with the software and ecosystem monetization optionality, justifies a sustained premium. The primary risks — export controls, customer concentration, and inventory overhang if AI capex momentum slows — are real and non-trivial, warranting disciplined position sizing rather than unconstrained conviction. Institutional investors with a 12–18 month horizon should view pullbacks toward the $195–$205 range as accumulation opportunities, while being prepared for continued volatility driven by policy headlines rather than fundamental deterioration. The base case remains a company in the early-to-middle innings of a multi-year infrastructure supercycle, with the 10-Q providing no evidence that thesis has changed.\n\n---\n\n**Sentiment Score: 8**	9	2026-06-01 06:24:04.319497	2026-06-01 06:13:12.858285	\N
